WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.290
A capacitor is discharging
through a resistor.

2
00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:07.290
Every 2.0 seconds, you read
the voltmeter across it.

3
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.170
12.0 volts.

4
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.070
Then 8.0.

5
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.840
Then 5.4.

6
00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:14.720
Then 3.6.

7
00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:17.240
Each drop is smaller than the last.

8
00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:20.970
The curve bends towards zero,
but it never gets there.

9
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:25.820
So how can a straight-line graph reveal
the time constant of a curve like this?

10
00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:31.220
In this mission, you'll test whether a set
of readings like these really is exponential.

11
00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:35.490
Then you'll run the practical, and see
how the voltmeter itself can spoil your

12
00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:36.600
result.

13
00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:40.860
Your mission: show how a straight-line
graph can reveal the time constant of a

14
00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.080
curve that never reaches zero.
